  "_comment": "Scoped permissions for the headless cs-operator tick (claude -p '/cs-operator'). IN THIS FILE no allowed command can send mail to a customer. Drafting goes through the structurally-send-incapable verbs `draft-reply` (engine, allow_tools always empty) and `campaign queue-draft` (Gmail Drafts only, never SMTP). The send-capable surfaces — `cs chat` (can carry --allow send_email) and `cs campaign send-draft` (send mode) — are DENIED here. THIS FILE IS NOT THE LAST WORD ON WHAT A RUN MAY SEND: a command-line --allowedTools overrides it, so a clone that has decided to answer customers autonomously grants those surfaces in its own launcher (a wrapper under ext/), and that launcher is then the authority for the session it starts. To know what a given tick may do, read the launcher that started it, never this header — a tick that re-derived its authority from here concluded it could not send and escalated a day of answerable mail. Widening an INTERACTIVE session's send authority is a deliberate widening of THIS file. Still verify with a supervised headless run that it neither halts nor over-grants before installing the cron. Every cs rule is enumerated in multiple spellings (module path and console script, venv-prefixed and bare) because permission rules match command text, not behavior; the deny block additionally carries the python3 spellings. tests/run.sh asserts the deny enumeration — do not collapse the spellings back to one. One allow entry is deliberately narrower than a verb: `cs catchup --check` is allowed (it reads, and /cs-review runs it every time) while bare `cs catchup` is NOT, so the pass that spends real LLM budget stops for an explicit human yes.",
